>YES! It is a joke and I loved it! 

>Schrodinger, one of the pioneers of Quantum Mechanics, made a metaphor,
>if a rather sadistic one, for what was later to be one of the postulates of QM. He presented the following:
>If you place a cat into a box and next to that cat you place a gun that will
>randomly fire and close the lid, you will not know condition of the cat 
>whether dead or alive untill you open the box and look. In other words, you
>cannot know the current state untill you make a measurement.

>The significance of this broadens out to include: if one takes a measurement
>then the measurement itself will effect the possible outcomes. So in fact, it 
>is impossible to determine what happened BEFORE measuring (checking whether
>the cat is dead or alive) since measuring would force the system to alow only
>certain possiblities (the gun might be influenced by you opening the box).


